# Is this installation working?
#
# The one resource a node can apply the moment the package is installed.  It
# reads nothing and writes nothing: the `noop` provider reports back exactly
# the state it was asked for, so this is always in sync.
#
#     $ detc apply --type resource noop/ping
#     ok      noop    ping
#
# What that proves is everything around the resource -- that the provider was
# found under one of its prefixes, that its schema parsed, that the declaration
# was expanded through the namespace, that a program was started with the right
# working directory and DETC_ROOT, and that its answer came back and compared
# equal.  Through `detctl`, that all of it works across the connection too.
#
# It says nothing about the probes, on purpose, and the two defaults below are
# what keep it that way: a node with no `os` probe still gets its `ok`, and
# finds out that a probe is missing from `detc check --type probe`, which is
# the question that asks it.  Undefined is an error while a template is being
# rendered, so `system.os.pretty_name` on a node without the probe would fail
# here and the failure would read as though detc itself were broken.
#
# The defaults have to be taken one level at a time.  A default on the whole
# chain does not rescue it: `system` being absent makes `system.os` the error,
# before anything downstream is reached.
{% set os = (system | default({})).os | default({}) -%}
message: "detc answers on {{ os.pretty_name | default('an unknown system') }}"
